Complete these steps in order before publishing a product collection.

The [contract reference](./DEVELOPMENT.md#boundaries) defines the adapter
fields and required checks used below.

1. Check metadata, licensing, dependencies, runtime floors and author handles.
2. Replace the invented existing-only fixture with a researched product
   resource. Keep the invariant files in
   [DEVELOPMENT.md](./DEVELOPMENT.md#boundaries) unchanged. Adapt the typed
   declarations in `contract_subject.py` with real information entry points and
   a fake adapter, and declare its mutation granularity.
3. Declare native write token sequences in `Contract.write_command`. Preserve
   real subprocess-start injection and unchanged/no-dispatch checks.
4. Declare accepted arguments, their literal argv elements and refused arguments
   in `Contract.literals`. Use real values for closed vocabularies; retain
   pre-dispatch validation and the separate executable transport cases.
5. Implement `inject_host_identity()` at the prepared execution boundary. Keep
   the public locator valid and test unsupported locator options separately.
6. Declare `Contract.environment_policy` and document inherited, module-level
   and target environments. Keep explicit locale and secret-value checks for
   both refusal and neutralization.
7. Declare each native fault independently in `Contract.faults`. Preserve effect
   evidence, redaction, no redispatch, actual deadlines and transport
   nondispatch.
8. Declare `expected` and `literal_expected` with exact `Expectations`,
   including partial state for fieldwise writes. Keep preservation and every
   readback assertion exact.
9. Keep observational subjects free of writers. Register a first mutator with
   its change, repeat, prediction and uncertainty cases in the same change.
   Creation and deletion require executable fixtures that exercise every new
   case.
10. Qualify the product's locator, including application identity, effective
    environment, one shared deadline and descendant cleanup. The example
    supports a trusted foreground program only.
11. State the native identity and concurrency guarantees. The fixture's
    conditional update under a file lock supplies no guarantee for a different
    product.
12. Set the compatibility series and exclusions from tested builds. Select the
    binding's compatibility policy and document it.
13. The engine remains shared for products with qualified definitions. This
    variant has no definition-driven CLI example. Its resource registry binds
    `FIXTURE` to the hook class; replace that registration with the product's
    resources.
14. The HTTP inventory extractor is dropped. Add a CLI inventory extractor only
    when a versioned product interface supplies the source, and retain source
    revision and extraction hashes beside its development output.
15. Adapt the real executable, seeded integration targets, process-fault inputs
    and managed-host scenario together. Keep the standalone module-process case
    crossing the actual utils subprocess boundary.
16. Update the README's execution, ownership, compatibility and usage
    statements. Replace its fixture qualification with the product builds and
    locators tested. Run the checks in
    [DEVELOPMENT.md](./DEVELOPMENT.md#checks), then remove this checklist.
